    {
      "passage_id": "e88bd4ce-1616-5f72-b6d6-c874bc4d1049",
      "slug": "completion-how-paper-is-recycled",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "Every year, hundreds of millions of tonnes of paper are thrown away, yet much of it\ncan be used again. Recycling turns old newspapers, boxes and office paper back into\nfresh sheets through a process that is surprisingly simple in outline.\n\nThe journey begins with collection. Waste paper is gathered, sorted by type, and then\nbroken down. To do this, workers mix the paper with water and chemicals, stirring it\nuntil it becomes a soft, wet pulp. This pulp is the raw material from which new paper\nwill be made.\n\nThe pulp is far from clean, however. It is passed through a series of screens that\nremove contaminants such as glue, plastic windows from envelopes, and metal staples.\nThe most important cleaning step comes next. In a stage known as de-inking, air\nbubbles are blown through the pulp; the ink sticks to the bubbles and floats to the\nsurface, where it can be skimmed away. What remains is a grey-white mixture of clean\nfibres.\n\nThese fibres are then pressed between rollers to squeeze out the water, dried, and\nwound into huge new rolls of paper, ready to be cut and printed once more.\n\nThe environmental case for all this effort is strong. Making paper from recycled\nfibre uses far less energy than making it from fresh wood, and because the fibres come\nfrom old paper rather than forests, fewer trees are cut down. There is one limit,\nthough: every time paper is recycled, the fibres grow a little shorter and weaker.\nMost paper can be recycled only about five to seven times before the fibres are simply\ntoo short to hold together — after which they are best returned, at last, to the earth.",
