      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "A baby penguin is called a chick. Like all birds, a penguin chick hatches from an egg. The\nparents take great care of their eggs before the big day.\n\nWhen a chick is born, it does not look like its parents. It is covered in soft, fluffy\nfeathers called down. These feathers are usually grey or brown. They keep the chick warm,\nbut they are not waterproof. This means a young chick cannot swim. If it went into the sea,\nit would get too cold.\n\nPenguin chicks are always hungry. The parents catch fish in the sea and bring the food\nback up for their chick to eat. A chick can eat a lot in one day!\n\nSome chicks join a big group of other young birds. This group is called a crèche. Inside\nthe group, the chicks stay warm and safe while their parents look for food.\n\nAfter some weeks, a chick grows new, waterproof feathers. Now it can swim and catch its\nown fish at last.",
