    {
      "passage_id": "40428ebe-03a9-57ac-a2be-f7cdcb135ada",
      "slug": "inference-the-bystander-effect",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "On a winter night in 1964, a young woman named Kitty Genovese was attacked and killed near her home in New York. The story that spread afterwards was even more disturbing than the crime itself: a newspaper reported that 38 respectable neighbours had watched from their windows and done nothing. The case became a symbol of urban coldness, yet much of what made it famous later turned out to be shaky.\n\nDecades on, researchers who returned to the records found little support for the tidy figure of 38. The number appears to have come from a remark by a police official and passed straight into print without close checking. Some neighbours had heard only fragments through closed windows on a cold night; at least two did contact the police, and one stayed with the dying woman. The myth, in short, was far neater than the messy truth behind it.\n\nWhat the case did achieve was to push two psychologists, John Darley and Bibb Latané, to ask why a group of onlookers might freeze. In one well-known experiment they let smoke seep into a room where students sat filling in forms. Alone, most reported it quickly; seated beside calm strangers who were secretly part of the study, far fewer spoke up, reading the strangers' stillness as a signal that nothing was wrong. The researchers traced this partly to diffusion of responsibility — the sense that, in a crowd, each onlooker assumes another will step in, so no single person feels obliged to.\n\nThe bystander effect is now one of the most cited ideas in social psychology, yet it has grown more cautious with age. Studies of real attacks caught on camera suggest that help does often arrive, particularly when the danger is plain or one person breaks the spell by acting first. The effect, on this reading, bends rather than dictates behaviour, and how strongly it grips any crowd may depend on the moment as much as on the number of people watching.",
