    {
      "passage_id": "09eecfa1-e2f7-5c01-bc24-8b4ffbc66655",
      "slug": "inference-the-ethics-of-privacy",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "Privacy was once a matter of walls and doors — of who could see into a room or read a sealed letter. The digital age has quietly dissolved those familiar boundaries, replacing them with a question that earlier generations rarely had to confront: how much of a life can be recorded, stored and analysed before something essential to personhood is lost?\n\nThe novelty of the present moment lies less in any single technology than in the sheer ordinariness of surveillance. A person need not consent to be observed in any meaningful sense; merely by carrying a phone, searching the web or paying for groceries, the individual scatters a trail of data that others quietly collect. What makes this troubling is not only the scale of the watching but its repurposing: information gathered to deliver a service is readily turned to ends — advertising, scoring, prediction — that the person never imagined when the trail was first laid.\n\nDefenders of the system are not without arguments. The capacity to amass and analyse data at scale has improved medicine, eased commerce and, in some accounts, made cities safer. The difficulty is that these gains accrue diffusely, to institutions and populations, while the costs — a loss of control, a creeping sense of exposure — fall on individuals who can rarely trace exactly how, or by whom, their information is being used. Consent, the traditional safeguard, becomes hollow when refusal would mean withdrawing from ordinary life altogether.\n\nRecent law has tried to restore some measure of control, most visibly through the so-called \"right to be forgotten\", established by a 2014 ruling of the Court of Justice of the European Union, which allows a person to demand that certain records be erased. Yet even this apparently modest remedy proves thorny in practice. One individual's wish to delete a past clashes, at times, with the public's interest in an accurate record of what occurred, and deciding whose claim should prevail is seldom a matter of simple rules.\n\nIt would be comforting to suppose that the right balance could be struck and then forgotten, fixed in a statute and left to run. The harder truth is that privacy names not a single value but a shifting compromise among several — autonomy, security, transparency, memory — whose weights change with circumstance and technology alike. No statute can settle such a question once and for all; it can only be argued out, again and again, in public, as the tools that test it continue to evolve.",
