    {
      "passage_id": "8a655492-c0a6-4b2d-a7b7-55ddeb860090",
      "slug": "matching-philosophers-of-mind",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "**A** Ask a neuroscientist what a thought is and one may receive a description of cells and electrical currents; ask a person undergoing a thought, and the answer is something quite different. The gap between those two kinds of account has haunted the philosophy of mind for centuries, and rival schools have formed largely around how that gap should be treated.\n\n**B** The most influential statement of the problem belongs to René Descartes, who argued that mind and body are two distinct kinds of substance — one thinking and immaterial, one extended and physical. This dualist view dominated for a long time, but it was eventually weakened by a puzzle it raised: if the mind is in no way physical, how can it act upon a body that is wholly physical?\n\n**C** Partly to sidestep that question, the functionalists proposed that what makes a state mental is not the stuff it is made of but the role it plays. On their view, a belief or a pain is defined by the job it does within a network of inputs, outputs and other states — much as a piece of software can run on different kinds of machine. The appeal of this framework was that it placed the mind firmly within the reach of science without appealing to a separate soul.\n\n**D** Not everyone, however, was persuaded that explaining function settled everything. David Chalmers argued famously that even a complete account of how the brain processes information would leave a separate question open: why is there something it feels like to be in such a state at all? He called this the \"hard problem\" of consciousness, and distinguished it from the easier \"easy problems\" of mapping mechanisms.\n\n**E** Daniel Dennett responded with pronounced scepticism. For him, the sense that some mysterious residue remains once all the functions are explained is itself the thing to be explained away, not revered — a confusion arising from how we talk about experience, and one that careful analysis will dissolve. What Chalmers takes to be a deep chasm, Dennett regards as an illusion.\n\n**F** None of these positions has driven the others from the field, and most contemporary work moves between them, borrowing the rigour of functionalism while still feeling the pull of Chalmers's question. Whether the gap between mechanism and experience will finally close or merely be redescribed in fresher terms remains a matter on which reasonable minds continue to disagree.",
      "expected_metadata": {
