      "source_body_markdown": "**A** The phrase 'renewable energy' is used as if it described a single thing, but it\nactually covers a group of very different technologies. What they share is not how they\nwork but a single feature: they all draw on sources that are naturally replenished,\nrather than burning fossil fuels that will run out.\n\n**B** The most familiar is solar power, turning sunlight directly into electricity.\nBecause solar panels have no moving parts and can be fitted onto rooftops that already\nexist, they can be installed where large power stations cannot reach. Their weakness is\nas plain as their appeal: they produce nothing at night and less under cloud, so their\noutput rises and falls with the weather.\n\n**C** Hydropower has a far longer history. It has generated electricity on a large\nscale for longer than any other renewable source, by letting falling water pass through\nturbines. The Hoover Dam in the United States, for instance, has a generating capacity\nof around 2,080 megawatts. It is reliable and powerful, but it comes at a cost: building a large dam can\ndrown valleys and force the communities living there to move.\n\n**D** Wind power, now growing fast around the world, uses large blades to catch moving\nair and turn a generator. Like solar, it is clean and plentiful but uneven, strong on\nwindy days and silent on still ones.\n\n**E** Geothermal power taps a source beyond the reach of most other technologies: the\nheat held deep inside the Earth. By pumping water down to hot rock, engineers raise\nsteam that can spin turbines. Iceland, sitting on especially active ground, now draws\naround a quarter of its electricity this way. The drawback is that it works best where the hot rock\nlies near the surface, often in volcanically active regions.\n\n**F** Finally, biomass energy burns plant matter — wood, straw or special crops — to\nmake heat and power. It is a special case, because the burning does release carbon\ndioxide. It counts as renewable only if new plants regrow to absorb a similar amount,\nclosing the cycle over time.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Cụm V-ing sau danh từ thường = mệnh đề quan hệ rút gọn — quy nó về danh từ đứng trước để biết nó mô tả nguồn năng lượng NÀO khi giải matching.",
            "point": "Mệnh đề quan hệ rút gọn dạng V-ing hậu bổ nghĩa",
            "review": "Mệnh đề quan hệ chủ động có thể rút gọn thành V-ing (a source turning X = a source that turns X).",
            "example": "The most familiar is solar power, **turning sunlight directly into electricity**.",
            "analysis": "'turning sunlight directly into electricity' là cụm phân từ hiện tại rút gọn (= which turns…), bổ nghĩa cho 'solar power'. Dạng rút gọn lược 'which' và 'be/động từ chia', chỉ giữ V-ing 'turning'. Cụm đứng sau dấu phẩy mang tính bổ sung."
