    {
      "passage_id": "eaedf0eb-ba3e-5ed1-8f2b-a3049f8ad062",
      "slug": "skimming-history-of-the-bicycle",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "# Skimming — The History of the Bicycle\n\n**A**\nThe bicycle that you see on the street today took many years to invent. In 1817, a German man named Karl von Drais built one of the very first machines with two wheels but no pedals. Although it had a seat and a bar to steer, a rider still had to push the ground with both feet to roll along.\n\n**B**\nAbout forty years later, inventors in France made the next big step. Pedals were added to the front wheel, so riders could turn the wheel with their feet instead of pushing the ground. This machine, which people called the \"boneshaker\", gave a bumpy ride because its wheels were hard iron.\n\n**C**\nIn the 1870s came the strange-looking penny-farthing, a machine with a very big front wheel and a tiny back wheel. The big wheel lifted the rider high up, which made the bike fast but dangerous. While experienced riders enjoyed the speed, beginners found it easy to fall off.\n\n**D**\nThen, in 1885, a British inventor named John Kemp Starley built the \"safety bicycle\". It had two wheels of the same size and a chain that turned the back wheel, so it was lower and safer than the penny-farthing. Because almost anyone could use it, this is the bicycle that we still ride today.\n\n---\n\n*Practice tip: When you skim, read the first sentence of each paragraph to find the main idea. You do not need to understand every word.*",
      "expected_metadata": {
