      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "For many visitors, a botanical garden is simply a pleasant place to stroll among\nlabelled plants. The earliest such gardens, though, were not created for enjoyment. The\nfirst university gardens in sixteenth-century Europe were laid out to grow medicinal and\nuseful plants for study, not as ornamental displays, and that tradition of serious\npurpose still shapes botanical gardens today.\n\nTheir clearest modern role is conservation. Botanical gardens hold living collections\nthat represent thousands of species, some of which are now extinct in the wild — they\nsurvive only in cultivation. By keeping such plants alive and reproducing, the gardens\npreserve genetic material that might one day be used to return a species to its natural\nhome. A handful of dramatic rescues, in which a plant has been bred up and replanted in\nthe landscape it had disappeared from, are often cited to show that the effort can pay\noff.\n\nIt is sometimes argued that frozen seed banks could now do this work more cheaply, but\nthe argument misses something. Seeds and living plants do different jobs: a frozen seed\ncannot be watched as it grows, flowers, or copes with pests and disease, and some\nspecies produce seeds that do not survive freezing well. Living collections also let\nresearchers study how a plant responds to drought or a warming climate, questions a\nsealed vault cannot answer. The two methods therefore complement each other rather than\nreplace one another.\n\nScientific work, however, is only part of the story. Botanical gardens also teach\nmillions of visitors about plants they would otherwise never notice, and that attention\ncan feed support for protecting habitats far away. This is why the verdict should\nperhaps be a conditional one: a botanical garden earns its keep when it joins research,\nconservation and public learning, not when it is merely a pleasant place to walk.",
      "expected_metadata": {
