      "slug": "the-future-of-farming-indoors",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "Inside a windowless **warehouse** near a major city, rows of lettuce grow in stacked\ntrays under pink and purple light. No soil, sunshine or rain reaches the plants, yet\nthey **thrive**. This is **indoor farming**, and supporters believe it could change\nwhere and how food is grown.\n\nIn these **vertical farm**s, **crop**s are grown in **layer**s piled one above another\nto save space. Instead of sunlight, energy-efficient LED lamps provide exactly the\n**wavelength**s plants need. Many systems use **hydroponics**, in which roots sit in\n**nutrient**-rich water rather than soil. Sensors track temperature, humidity and\ncarbon dioxide and adjust them minute by minute. Because the climate is fully\ncontrolled, **harvest**s continue all year, unaffected by weather.\n\nThe benefits can be striking. By recycling its water, an indoor farm can use up to 95\npercent less than a traditional field. Growing food close to consumers cuts the\ndistance **produce** must travel, so it arrives fresher. Without open fields, crops\nalso need few or no **pesticide**s, since insects are kept outside.\n\nThe approach has serious limits. Running thousands of lamps and pumps **consume**s\nlarge amounts of electricity, which can be both costly and polluting if the power is\nnot clean. So far, only high-value crops such as leafy greens and herbs are profitable\nindoors. **Staple** foods like wheat and rice remain far cheaper to grow outdoors. Building such\na farm also demands far more money upfront than ploughing an open field.\n\nWhether indoor farming becomes a mainstream source of food may depend on the price of\n**renewable** energy. For now, it is best seen as a useful **supplement** to ordinary\n**agriculture** rather than a replacement. Still, in crowded or dry regions, the\nvertical farm offers an intriguing glimpse of one possible future.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Một câu có thể chứa cả bị động chính lẫn phân từ rút gọn; tách từng cụm để biết ai/cái gì chịu hành động nào.",
            "point": "Câu bị động hiện tại + cụm phân từ rút gọn",
            "review": "Bị động: S + be + V3. Rút gọn mệnh đề quan hệ bị động: N + (which/that are) + V3 → N + V3.",
            "example": "crops **are grown** in layers **piled** one above another",
            "analysis": "'are grown' là bị động hiện tại đơn (be + V3), tác nhân (người trồng) bị lược. 'piled one above another' là cụm quá khứ phân từ rút gọn từ 'layers that are piled…', bổ nghĩa cho 'layers' với nghĩa bị động."
