      "passage_id": "70cd8700-bf0f-5459-862f-d2766ee647e7",
      "slug": "the-great-barrier-reef",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "Off the north-east **coast** of Australia lies a living wonder so large that\nastronauts can see it from space. The Great Barrier Reef is the world's biggest\n**coral reef** system. It **stretches** for more than 2,300 kilometres along the\nwarm, shallow sea.\n\nAlthough it looks like rock, the reef is alive. It is built by millions of **tiny**\nanimals called coral polyps. Over thousands of years, their hard skeletons join\ntogether to form the giant structure we see today.\n\nThe reef is one of the richest places for wildlife on the planet. It offers food and\n**shelter** to about 9,000 **species**, including colourful fish, sharks, turtles and\ndolphins. This natural beauty also **attracts** visitors from every continent.\n\nSadly, the reef now faces a growing **threat**. As the sea grows warmer, the coral can\nlose its colour and turn white. This process is called **bleaching**, and if it\ncontinues for too long, the coral dies. **Pollution** from the land and powerful\nstorms make the problem worse.\n\nPeople are trying hard to **protect** the reef. Scientists watch the water closely,\nand the government has created rules to limit damage. Coral can sometimes **recover**\nif the water cools again in time.\n\nWhether the reef can survive a warmer future is still uncertain, but it remains one of\nthe most precious places on the planet.",
