      "passage_id": "139751d2-e567-56fe-a46e-f888a2e014c6",
      "slug": "the-invention-of-photography",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In 1826, a Frenchman named Joseph Niépce pointed a wooden box at the view from his\nwindow and waited for many hours. The blurry result was the world's first surviving\nphotograph. It marked the **invention** of a powerful new technology that would soon\nlet anyone freeze a moment in time.\n\nEarly cameras could only **capture** an image very slowly. Light fell onto a treated\nmetal or glass **surface**, but the **exposure** lasted several minutes. For that\nreason, a person sitting for a **portrait** had to keep perfectly still. Many\nphotographs from the period therefore show stiff, serious faces. Having a picture\ntaken meant visiting a special **studio**, and the service was expensive. As a result,\nit remained a treat for the wealthy.\n\nThis changed near the end of the century. In 1888, the American George Eastman sold a\nsimple camera loaded with a **roll** of film, and his methods made photography\n**affordable**. Suddenly an ordinary **amateur** could take dozens of pictures. The\nwhole camera was then sent away, and a laboratory would **develop** the film and\nreturn the prints. Photography was no longer only for experts.\n\nA century later, a far bigger shift arrived. **Digital** cameras stored pictures as\nelectronic files, and these new devices quickly began to **replace** film. Today\nalmost everyone carries a camera inside a phone. Even so, some photographers argue\nthat the old film images may still hold a warmth that screens cannot quite match.",
