      "slug": "the-paradox-of-expert-intuition",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In 2009, two psychologists who had spent careers disagreeing — Daniel Kahneman, a sceptic\nof human judgement, and Gary Klein, a champion of it — published a paper jointly asking\nwhen expert **intuition** can be trusted.\n\nThe puzzle they confronted is genuine. A seasoned firefighter who suddenly orders a\nretreat moments before a floor collapses appears to possess a near-magical **expertise**,\nyet the same confident **snap judgement**, made by a stock-picker, may be worthless. The\ndifficulty lies in telling skilled insight from lucky guesswork. Decades of research had\nfailed to settle the matter, partly because a confident specialist and a confident charlatan\ncan sound, from the outside, almost indistinguishable.\n\nTheir shared answer was that intuition is merely recognition. What looks like inspiration\nis, in Klein's account, the rapid **retrieval** of patterns laid down by experience; the\nexpert does not reason toward a conclusion so much as perceive it. Kahneman accepted this,\nbut insisted that such pattern **recognition** can only develop under particular\nconditions. Stripped of its mystique, intuition becomes something one can test and bound,\nrather than something to be uncritically admired or reflexively feared.\n\nThey agreed on two prerequisites. The first is a **regular** environment, one whose\npatterns are stable enough to be genuinely predictable rather than random; the second is\nprolonged **deliberate practice** with rapid, unambiguous **feedback**, so that the\nlearner can absorb those regularities. Neither condition alone suffices, since practice in\na chaotic world teaches very little and a predictable world rewards no one who has never\ntrained patiently within it.\n\nThis neatly explains the paradox: chess masters and anaesthetists operate in\n**high-validity** settings and earn their intuitions, whereas long-range political\nforecasters, working in irreducibly noisy domains, often do not — however confident they\nfeel. Confidence, the authors caution, is a feeling, not evidence; **overconfidence**\nflourishes precisely where genuine **regularities** are scarcest.\n\nWhat the collaboration could not fully resolve is how reliably anyone can judge, in\nadvance, whether a given **domain** is regular enough to trust. Until that question is\nsettled, the authors suggest, the wisest stance toward a confident expert may be neither\n**deference** nor **dismissal**, but a careful attempt to **calibrate** the trust the\nenvironment actually warrants.",
