    {
      "passage_id": "9c4804df-9178-5cc2-8d1a-64bf03e4333e",
      "slug": "the-power-of-first-impressions",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In a well-known experiment at Princeton University, volunteers were shown the faces\nof strangers for only a fraction of a second and asked to rate them. The\nresearchers found that a **first impression** of a face was formed in roughly a\ntenth of a second, after an exposure of just 100 milliseconds, and that giving\npeople longer to look barely changed their verdict. The judgement, it seems, is almost immediate.\n\nWithin that brief window, the brain assigns a whole set of **traits**. It decides\nhow likeable, competent and attractive a stranger appears, and above all it\nestimates **trustworthiness**. All of this happens in a **split second**, long\nbefore any words are exchanged. The mind draws on visible **cues** such as facial\nexpression, posture and tone of voice, and from these fragments it rapidly\n**assesses** whether the person is a friend or a threat.\n\nThe trouble is that an early opinion does not simply fade once more information\narrives. Instead it creates a lasting **bias**. Having decided that someone is warm\nor cold, people tend to notice the details that **confirm** that view and quietly\n**overlook** anything that points the other way. A first judgement therefore tends\nto reinforce itself.\n\nSuch instant reactions are not worthless. Research suggests that snap judgements can\nbe surprisingly **accurate**, particularly when a person is relaxed and behaving\nnaturally. Yet the same speed makes them **misleading**, because a single nervous\nmoment, an unfamiliar accent or an awkward silence may distort the picture entirely.\nOnce formed, a strong impression is difficult to **revise**, and doing so takes real\nmental effort.\n\nThe lesson drawn from this work is cautious rather than simple. A brief\n**encounter** may indeed reveal something genuine about a stranger, but treating that\nflash of judgement as the final truth would, on the evidence available, be unwise.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Câu mở đầu bằng đối tượng thí nghiệm thường ở thể bị động — chú ý ai 'được làm gì', câu hỏi detail hay khai thác chi tiết này.",
            "point": "Bị động quá khứ đơn + lược tác nhân",
            "review": "Bị động quá khứ: S + was/were + V3. Có thể lược 'by…'; sau một số động từ bị động (be asked/told/shown) dùng to-infinitive.",
            "example": "volunteers **were shown** the faces of strangers… and **asked** to rate them",
            "analysis": "'were shown' và '(were) asked' là bị động quá khứ đơn (were + V3), chủ ngữ 'volunteers' là người chịu tác động; tác nhân (các nhà nghiên cứu) được lược bỏ vì văn phong học thuật ưu tiên đối tượng của thí nghiệm. 'asked to rate' kèm to-infinitive làm bổ ngữ."
