    {
      "passage_id": "801cc193-8c83-5943-bf89-014ce22ffff4",
      "slug": "the-rise-of-plant-based-diets",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "Twenty years ago, a meal without meat was often seen as dull or lacking in goodness.\nToday, more and more people are choosing **plant-based** meals, and supermarket\nshelves are full of soya milk and bean burgers. In Portugal alone, the number of\nvegetarians rose by around 400 per cent in a single decade. This **shift** is driven\nby several concerns, not just one.\n\nThe first reason is the environment. Studies show that the **footprint** of plants on\nthe planet is far smaller than that of meat. The **greenhouse gas** emissions per gram\nof **protein** from beef are far higher than those from **legume**s, which also use\nless water and land to grow. For this reason, some governments have even encouraged\npeople to **moderate** the amount of meat they eat. China, for example, has urged its\nhuge population to cut meat consumption sharply over the coming years, partly to lower\nthe country's emissions.\n\nThe second reason is health. A diet rich in legumes, nuts and whole grains supplies\nplenty of **nutrient**s, and eating more plants is **associated with** a lower risk of\n**cardiovascular** disease, some cancers and early death. However, \"plant-based\" does\nnot automatically mean healthy: some **processed** **substitute**s are high in salt,\nsugar or fat, and may be little better than the products they replace. Reading the\nlabel matters as much as the word on the front of the packet.\n\nFor this reason, experts tend to advise a **balanced** approach rather than strict\nrules. Many people do not give up meat completely; they simply **adopt** more\nplant-based meals each week, a habit sometimes called \"flexitarian\" eating. Cooks are\nalso finding that beans, lentils and grains can be tasty rather than dull. Whether the\ntrend will keep accelerating remains unclear, but for many families the plant-based\nplate appears to be slowly becoming normal.",
