    {
      "passage_id": "fc2c4dcb-8cca-565b-a5e9-4693ecea1477",
      "slug": "the-science-of-fingerprints",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In 1892, police in Argentina solved a murder using a bloody mark left on a door\nframe — one of the first cases ever cracked by a **fingerprint**. That single\nprint did more than identify a suspect; it helped launch a science that still\nshapes criminal investigation today.\n\nA fingerprint is formed by the **pattern** of raised lines, or **ridges**, that cover\nthe tip of each finger. This pattern is not random. The ridges begin to form\nin the **womb**, shaped by the way the skin grows and presses against the\nsurrounding tissue. Because that process is influenced by countless tiny factors,\nthe result is **unique** to every person. The ridges are not only a marker of\nidentity, however; they also help the hand **grip** smooth surfaces and improve the\nsense of touch.\n\nThe most striking fact is that even **identical** twins, who share the same genes,\nhave different fingerprints. This is what makes prints so valuable as\n**forensic** evidence. When a finger touches a surface, it leaves behind sweat and\noil in the shape of the ridges. Such marks, called **latent** prints, are usually\ninvisible, so investigators brush fine **powder** over the surface to make them\nappear. The recovered print can then become **evidence** in court.\n\nIn the past, an officer had to compare prints by eye, a slow and difficult job.\nToday a print is scanned and checked automatically against a huge **database**,\nreturning possible matches in seconds. The same technology now sits in everyday\nlife: the small **scanner** on a phone reads a finger to unlock the screen.\n\nFingerprints are not a perfect answer, since prints can be smudged and matches\nmust still be confirmed by experts. Even so, more than a century after that first\ncase, the lines on our fingers remain one of the most **reliable** ways yet found\nto tell one person from another.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "'Because' báo hiệu quan hệ nhân–quả, chỗ hay ra câu hỏi 'tại sao/nguyên nhân' — bám vào mệnh đề sau 'because'.",
            "point": "Mệnh đề trạng ngữ nguyên nhân với 'Because' + mệnh đề chính bị động",
            "review": "Because + clause = vì. Khác 'because of + N'. Bị động giữ 'by + tác nhân' khi tác nhân mang thông tin quan trọng.",
            "example": "**Because** that process is influenced by countless tiny factors, the result is unique to every person",
            "analysis": "'Because + S + V' nêu nguyên nhân, đứng đầu nên có dấu phẩy. Mệnh đề phụ chứa bị động 'is influenced by countless tiny factors' (be + V3 + by O — tác nhân được giữ lại). Mệnh đề chính 'the result is unique…' nêu kết quả."
