    {
      "passage_id": "128d1e62-07b7-5250-94bd-b09acedb4215",
      "slug": "the-science-of-laughter",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "A baby will often laugh before it can speak a single word. Laughter, it seems, is one of the earliest and most universal human behaviours, yet scientists are still working out exactly what it is for.\n\nAt its simplest, laughter is a physical **reflex**. A surprising joke or a playful situation can **trigger** a burst of sound and movement that is difficult to produce on command. Many researchers argue that this response is older than language itself, and that it **evolved** as a way to signal safety and goodwill within a group.\n\nThat social function may be the key. A shared **bout** of laughter creates a sense of **bonding**, telling others that no threat is present. Laughter is also strongly **contagious**: a single laugh can pass through a room, drawing in people who never heard the original joke. Genuine, **spontaneous** laughter is hard to fake, which is part of why it is so trusted as a signal. In one well-known study, the researcher Robert Provine found that speakers laugh about 46 per cent more often than the people listening to them.\n\nThere are physical effects as well. Laughter releases **endorphins**, the brain chemicals associated with pleasure and reduced pain. At the same time, a good laugh appears to lower **stress** by reducing the level of certain **hormones** in the body. Some scientists have even suggested a link between a positive, humorous mood and a stronger **immune system**.\n\nThese health claims should be treated with care. The differences between real and forced laughter can be **subtle**, and many studies are small, which makes firm conclusions hard to reach. For now, the medical benefits remain **tentative**, and further research is needed before laughter can be prescribed like a medicine.\n\nWhat is clear is that laughter rarely happens alone. People are far more likely to laugh in company than in private, a pattern that points back to its possible origin as a social glue holding human groups together.",
