      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In the 19th century, the city of New York had become so **crowded** that its leaders\nworried about people's health. In 1857, they **set aside** a large piece of land in\nthe middle of the island and turned it into Central Park. The idea was simple but\nnew: even a crowded city needs a **green space** where people can breathe.\n\nToday most large cities **design** their parks carefully. Trees do more than look\nbeautiful. They absorb harmful gases from passing cars, so they help to lower the\nlevel of **pollution**. The shade from trees also helps the streets **cool down** in\nsummer, when concrete roads hold a lot of heat. The leaves quietly **absorb** these\ngases all day long.\n\nParks are good for people in other ways too. Every morning, many people come to\n**exercise**, jog, or take a walk. At the weekend, families **gather** to play and\nrest. Even a small pond or a row of trees can support local **wildlife**, such as\nbirds and insects.\n\nOf course, a park does not look after itself. Workers must **maintain** the grass and\nthe flower beds, clear away litter, and repair the paths. This costs money. Even so,\nmost residents **value** the park near their home, and some studies suggest that\nhouses close to a park can be worth a little more. For these reasons, parks will\nprobably remain an important part of city life.",
