    {
      "passage_id": "8f41d0d4-0ff9-5c87-9a12-23089e314b4f",
      "slug": "ynng-can-machines-be-creative",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "In 2016, a portrait in the style of Rembrandt was unveiled in Amsterdam — every brushstroke generated by software trained on the master's surviving work. \"The Next Rembrandt\", as it was called, struck some viewers as a marvel and others as an elaborate forgery. The unease it provoked points to a question that newer image-generators have only sharpened: can a machine be genuinely creative, or merely appear to be?\n\nPart of the difficulty is that we have never agreed what creativity is. The cognitive scientist Margaret Boden draws a useful distinction between merely combining familiar ideas in a new way and the rarer feat of transforming the very space of possibilities — of producing something that could not have arisen under the old rules at all. By the first, looser standard, today's systems clearly qualify; by the second, the verdict is far less obvious, and I would be wary of conceding it too quickly.\n\nA familiar objection, older than the machines themselves, runs back to Ada Lovelace, who insisted that a computing engine can do only whatever we know how to order it to perform. On this view, a model trained on millions of human pictures originates nothing; it remixes, however dazzlingly, what people made before. Yet I am not convinced the objection settles the matter, since much human art is also recombination, and we do not on that account deny it the name of creativity.\n\nWhat seems to me harder to wave away is the question of intention. When a painter labours over a canvas, the work issues from a life — from things wanted, feared, remembered — whereas a generative model has no inner states for its output to express. It produces images that are about nothing, in the sense that nothing was meant by them. Whether that absence disqualifies the result, or whether meaning is something audiences supply rather than artists transmit, is exactly where reasonable people part company.\n\nMy own view is provisional. These systems are extraordinary instruments, and in the hands of an artist who selects, edits and reframes their output, they can plainly take part in creative work. But to credit the software itself with creativity — as though it shared our reasons for caring how a picture turns out — strikes me as premature. I would not rule it out for ever; I would only say that we are not there yet, and that we should be suspicious of how eagerly the headlines insist we are.",
      "expected_metadata": {
